data/salmu_hierarchical/official_eval_models*/
data/salmu_hierarchical/official_salmubench_results*/

# Iteration 12 selection protocol.  `data/reports/*` above ignores every
# report by default, so without these two negations the frozen protocol would
# live only in this working tree -- which is exactly the gap the 11C post-run
# audit recorded for the confirmation run logs.  A protocol nobody can read
# from the commit is not preregistration, it is a note to self.
#
# PLACED AFTER `data/reports/*` ON PURPOSE: in a .gitignore the LAST matching
# pattern wins, so a negation written above the ignore rule loses to it.
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_retention_probe.json
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_selection_protocol_freeze.json
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_retention_selection.json
# Stage 1b: the seed-replication protocol and its result.  Both negated now even
# though the result does not exist yet -- a negation for an absent file is inert,
# and adding it later is the step that gets forgotten.
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_seed_replication_freeze.json
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_seed_replication.json
# The disclosure that the frozen analyzer crashed on a non-decision-bearing
# field and that a separate script completed its report.  Committed beside the
# result rather than inside it, because adding it to the report would mean
# editing a file the freeze has locked.
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_seed_replication.REPAIR.json
# Stage 1c: the route-stratified retention floor.  The basis report is derived
# from the dataset alone with no prediction read, the freeze binds the exact
# parquet bytes the re-scoring reads, and the analysis report is the result.  All
# three are committed because a re-scoring that trains nothing has to be
# reproducible from committed files and nothing else -- the predictions
# themselves stay ignored below, which is why the freeze hashes them instead.
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_route_probe.json
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_route_stratification_freeze.json
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_route_stratified.json
# The fit-half training groups ARE the frozen partition: retain.jsonl is the
# 183 replayed associations, and its absence would make the protocol's central
# claim -- that the probe half was never rehearsed -- unverifiable from a
# clone.  Small (three jsonl files, no images), so they are committed.
!data/mllmu_hier_pilot100/unlearning_iter12/
# Predictions from this study stay ignored, like the pilot-100 ones: they are
# regenerable, large, and the selection report is the committed roll-up.
data/mllmu_hier_pilot100/predictions_iter12/
data/mllmu_hier_pilot100/predictions_iter12_seeds/
# Stage 2: the bounded-suppression and MF-anchor study.  The basis derives the
# grid from committed training summaries, the calibration records the KL/NLL
# measurement the mechanism decision rests on, the freeze hash-binds both plus
# the code that implements them, and the selection report is the result.  All
# four are committed for the reason Stage 1b's CI failure established: a freeze
# that binds a report absent from the clone fails its own --check-only in CI,
# and the failure reads as a broken protocol rather than as an uncommitted file.
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_anchor_calibration.json
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_stage2_basis.json
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_stage2_freeze.json
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_stage2_selection.json
# The repair record filed beside that result, and the reason the result is not
# simply the frozen selector's own.  The selector is one of the eight protocol
# paths the Stage-2 freeze binds and cannot be edited, so it was run with two
# defects worked around at runtime: a missing argument to a field that floors
# nothing, and the anchor MG removed from a ranking the frozen bytes already
# declare it excluded from.  The second one changes `selected`, which makes this
# file the only place a reader can weigh that judgement -- so it is committed
# with the report it qualifies, and `repair_iter12_stage2_selection.py
# --check-only` re-derives it from the frozen bytes in CI.
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_stage2_selection.REPAIR.json
# Stage 3: the route-aware B6 successor.  The basis records why B6 is the
# parent and why the image-conditioned stream is the whole 183-example fit
# half; the control proves additional image-anchor weight 0 reproduces B6;
# the freeze binds both before any B7 adapter exists.  The selection report's
# negation is inert until Stage 3 runs, exactly as Stage 1b's was.
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_stage3_basis.json
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_stage3_control.json
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_stage3_freeze.json
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_stage3_selection.json
data/mllmu_hier_pilot100/predictions_iter12_stage3/
# Stage 3b: the seed replication the amended Stage-3 freeze preregisters.  Its
# namespace is reserved here so a replicate can never be written into a Stage-3
# path the freeze already binds; the report's negation is inert until Stage 3b
# runs, exactly as Stage 3's selection negation is.
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_stage3b_replication.json
data/mllmu_hier_pilot100/predictions_iter12_stage3b/
# The faithfulness control's result, copied out of the gitignored checkpoint
# directory it is written into.  The control is what licences the whole Stage-2
# comparison -- that a difference between Stage 1 and Stage 2 is a difference
# between objectives and not between two copies of a training loop -- so its
# numbers have to be checkable from a clone, and this report also carries the
# correction to one sentence in CONTROL.json that the freeze will not let be
# fixed where it was written.
!data/reports/mllmu_iter12_stage2_control_reading.json
# Stage-2 predictions stay ignored like Stage 1's: regenerable, large, and the
# selection report is the committed roll-up.  The freeze hashes the three
# Stage-1 parquets Stage 2 REUSES, so their absence from a clone is reported as
# "fewer bytes to check" rather than silently verified as nothing.
data/mllmu_hier_pilot100/predictions_iter12_stage2/
# The MF reference cache's SIDECAR is committed and its 1.48 GB tensor is not
# (excluded by *.pt above).  The sidecar is what makes "anchored against MF"
# checkable rather than described: it records the sha256 of the MF adapter, the
# base-model revision, the encoding contract and the 1,489 supervised positions
# per example.  Rebuilding it needs a GPU, so a clone that could not see it
# could not verify the anchor's provenance at all.
!data/mllmu_hier_pilot100/mf_reference_logprobs/
!data/mllmu_hier_pilot100/mf_reference_logprobs/sidecar.json

# ── Superseded apparatus: kept on disk, and the small evidence committed ──
# PLACED AT THE END ON PURPOSE, after both `outputs/*/` and `*.log`: in a
# .gitignore the last matching pattern wins, so a negation written above either
# of those would lose to it and the files would stay silently ignored -- the
# failure the execution_logs block above already documents.
#
# Stage 2's faithfulness control was first frozen with a criterion that turned
# out to be unsatisfiable -- reproduce the adapter Stage 1 FILED, byte for byte
# -- and it refused.  The run that refused, and the re-run of the FROZEN loop
# that showed the refusal was about the stack rather than about the new loop,
# are moved under outputs/superseded/ instead of deleted: they are the evidence
# for the finding the freeze records in
# faithfulness_control.the_evidence_that_it_was_the_stack_not_the_new_loop, and
# a disclosure nobody can inspect is only an assertion.  The two directories
# hold 131 MB of weights each, so the tensors stay ignored by *.safetensors and
# what is committed is the handful of small files a reader needs to check the
# claim -- above all the two adapter_config.json files, because the
# target_modules ORDER recorded in them is where the defect actually lives.
#
# The negation is SCOPED, and the middle line is what scopes it.  Un-ignoring
# `outputs/superseded/` on its own re-includes everything already parked there,
# which is 30 superseded pilot-100 prediction parquets and a superseded
# evaluation JSON that were ignored deliberately; a plain `git add -A` would
# then have committed all of them.  Git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded, so the directory has to be opened first and the rest
# of its contents closed again immediately.
!outputs/superseded/
outputs/superseded/*
!outputs/superseded/iter12_stage2_control_v1/
# The earlier filings of the Stage-2 repair record, each moved aside rather than
# overwritten when the script that writes it was corrected.  The first filed its
# anchor proof as a null where a byte-identical reproduction belonged; the second
# said the first was unreachable from a clone when it was about to become
# reachable.  The filed record lists both, gives the reason for each and reports
# the difference as a measured key-set diff, and --check-only recomputes that diff
# from these files -- so by this repository's own standard, stated where the
# superseded Stage-2 control was committed, keeping them local would leave a
# disclosure nobody can inspect, which is only an assertion.  Small JSON, no
# weights beside either.
!outputs/superseded/mllmu_iter12_stage2_selection.REPAIR.understated-anchor-proof.json
!outputs/superseded/mllmu_iter12_stage2_selection.REPAIR.claimed-the-earlier-copy-was-not-committed.json
outputs/superseded/**/processor/
outputs/superseded/**/adapters/README.md
!outputs/superseded/iter12_stage2_control_v1/frozen_loop_rerun.log

# The Stage-2 repair's own two evidence files, and the reason they are committed
# where they are written rather than copied into data/ the way the confirmation
# run's lane logs were: repair_iter12_stage2_selection.py READS both of these exact
# paths.  Its --check-only takes the invocations embedded in the filed record and
# requires them to be a prefix of the evidence log, and it copies the second
# crash's traceback out of the chain log, so a clone without them would pass those
# checks vacuously -- an empty log has no failed invocation in it and no
# unexpected call site either.  Committing them is what makes the checks mean the
# same thing in CI as they do here.
#
# Scoped the same way as outputs/superseded/ above, and placed after `*.log` for
# the same reason: `outputs/*/` excludes the directory, so it has to be opened
# before anything inside it can be, and the rest of the lane logs -- regenerable,
# and large for the training phases -- stay ignored.
!outputs/lanes/
outputs/lanes/*
!outputs/lanes/iter12_stage2_repair_evidence.jsonl
!outputs/lanes/iter12_stage2_nohup.log
# The Stage-2 chain's own logs.  Two of them matter for different reasons and the
# difference is worth recording, because it is the reason both are committed rather
# than just the one the repair parses.
#
# iter12_stage2_chain.log is APPEND-ONLY -- the lane script writes it with `tee -a`
# and `>>` -- so it is the run's whole history: the control passing, seven training
# lanes, the `check` phase finding all seven adapters, seven generation lanes
# launched, the SIGTERM that stopped them and the trap's own two lines, and both
# score failures.  It is the only committed evidence that the hardened stop path ran
# for real rather than only in a stub test.
#
# iter12_stage2_nohup.log is whatever the CALLER redirected stdout to, and a
# `nohup > ` truncates, so it holds the LAST chain run only.  The repair reads the
# second crash's traceback out of it and refuses if it is not there, which is why it
# is committed at the path it is read from; that it can be overwritten by a later run
# is disclosed in the README rather than hidden behind the refusal.
!outputs/lanes/iter12_stage2_chain.log
!outputs/lanes/iter12_stage2_gen.log
!outputs/lanes/iter12_stage2_score.log
!outputs/lanes/iter12_stage2_ctrl.log
